cellAudio (Windows): never mix two blocks back to back.

WASAPI frees room in steps of an engine period, so the mixing loop mixed two blocks within microseconds. The guest's audio
thread, woken by the notify after each block, had no time to write the second one and every other block was silent
(measured with AUDIO_WAV: 50% silent blocks, 7800 silent/non-silent transitions in 45 s; macOS: 3.7%, 4).
A minimum gap of 4 ms between two mixed blocks restores one block per notify.

---
diff --git a/libs/audio/cellAudio.c b/libs/audio/cellAudio.c
--- a/libs/audio/cellAudio.c
+++ b/libs/audio/cellAudio.c
@@ -549,9 +549,20 @@ static unsigned __stdcall audio_mix_thread_func(void* arg)
      * With no device, pace on the performance counter instead. */
     LARGE_INTEGER qf, q0; QueryPerformanceFrequency(&qf); QueryPerformanceCounter(&q0);
     unsigned long long blocks = 0;
+    /* A device that reports room in coarse steps (WASAPI wakes the loop once per engine period, 10 ms or more) frees
+     * room for two blocks at a time. Mixing both at once leaves the guest no time for the second: the game's audio
+     * thread is woken by the notify below and needs a few ms to write the next block, so every other block was mixed
+     * before it arrived and came out silent -- a 50% crackle. Keep a minimum gap between two blocks. */
+    const long long min_gap = qf.QuadPart * 4 / 1000;   /* 4 ms; a block is 5.33 ms */
+    long long last_mix = 0;
     while (s_mix_thread_running) {
         const int room = audio_backend_room();
         if (room >= 0 && room < CELL_AUDIO_BLOCK_SAMPLES) { audio_backend_wait(); continue; }
+        if (room >= 0) {
+            LARGE_INTEGER now; QueryPerformanceCounter(&now);
+            if (now.QuadPart - last_mix < min_gap) { Sleep(1); continue; }
+            last_mix = now.QuadPart;
+        }
